    Replace json-simple with Gson (#7944)
    
    > [!NOTE]
    > This patch is one I wanted for the last 10ish years. Why do we have
    multiple JSON parser? No one knows! Also, `json-simple` is unmaintained
    for over a decade…
    
    `com.googlecode.json-simple` 1.1.1 has been unmaintained since 2012 and
    is not an OSGi bundle, so it
    had to be pulled into the Karaf feature behind a `wrap:` prefix. It was
    the second most widespread
    JSON library in the code base. This replaces every use of it with Gson
    and removes the dependency.
    
    The 107 source files across 33 modules that used it all move;
    `org.json.simple` no longer appears
    anywhere in the tree, and `dependency:tree` finds no trace of it in the
    reactor. The diff is larger
    than that because the `Jsons`/`JsonObj` DSL that wrapped json-simple
    goes with it.
    
    Deliberately out of scope: `MediaPackageParser.getAsJSON()` and
    `RestPublisher.OpencastJSONProvider`
    are frozen Jettison wire formats and keep using Jettison. Jackson stays
    where it is, in GraphQL, YAML
    and the LMS client DTOs.
    
    ### Notes for reviewers
    
    Most of this is mechanical, but a few things are not.
    
    **The signed URL policy is now written out by hand** rather than handed
    to a JSON library
    (`b9c9507`). Its exact bytes are part of our contract with anything that
    has ever signed a URL:
    `getPolicySignature` digests the JSON text, and the verifier re-renders
    the decoded policy and
    compares digests. Re-ordering a key or changing an escape would
    invalidate every signed URL in
    circulation and break third-party signers. Writing it by hand means the
    format cannot drift when a
    dependency is upgraded or swapped. Output was verified byte-identical
    across 144 generated policies,
    and `PolicyUtilsTest` pins the escaping.
    
    **A shared, strict Gson instance** (`GsonUtil`) overrides four Gson
    defaults that are wrong for
    Opencast: HTML escaping (we serialize URLs everywhere), dropping null
    members (the External API
    requires empty fields to be present), reading every number as `Double`,
    and lenient parsing. The last
    one matters most: without it a malformed request body would quietly
    become a 200 instead of a 400.
    
    **Bugs found and fixed on the way.** These are separate commits and can
    be reviewed on their own:
    
    * `930430b` — `SeriesServiceRemoteImpl.getSeriesProperties` and
    `getSeriesElements` parsed with
    json-simple and cast the result to jettison's `JSONArray`. Those are
    unrelated types, so both casts
    always threw `ClassCastException`, which the surrounding catch turned
    into a `SeriesException`.
      Neither method could ever return a value.
    * `c98e2c0d` — `ServicesEndpoint.Service` carried two renderings of
    itself, a Gson `toJSON()` and a
    json-simple `toJSONString()` reached through `JSONAware`. Only the first
    is ever called, and the two
    had drifted: the dead one stringified every number, dropped the status
    translation prefix, and
    passed nulls through. This was Opencast's only `JSONAware`
    implementation, so the interface leaves
      the code base with it.
    * `6e745e91` — `PlaylistRestService.parseJsonToPlaylist` declared
    json-simple's `ParseException` but
    parses with Jackson and never threw it. That declaration alone pulled
    json-simple into three
      modules.
    * `fe8763ef` — `security-aai` had no JUnit dependency of its own.
    json-simple 1.1.1 declares
    `junit` at compile scope, so its tests were compiling against the copy
    that leaked through.
    
    **Two test fixtures were never valid JSON** and only worked because
    json-simple accepted them:
    `TestEventEndpoint`'s access policy had a stray trailing quote, and
    `usersettings.json` was missing a
    comma. Both are fixed rather than worked around.
    
    **One deliberate behaviour change.**
    `ServiceRegistryEndpoint.removeJobs` had no error handling around
    parsing its `jobIds` parameter, so a malformed value became a 500. It
    answers 400 now, and the
    `@RestQuery` documents that response.
    
    **One removal that needed care.** `basiclti-util` pulls in json-simple
    1.1 transitively, and the
    `dependencyManagement` entry had been quietly pinning that up to 1.1.1
    for the whole build. None of
    `basiclti-util`'s class files reference `org/json/simple`; its own pom
    simply declares a dependency it
    does not use, so it is excluded at that one call site.
    
    ### How to test this patch
    
    No configuration or migration is required. Existing serialized ACLs and
    other stored JSON keep working
    — only the key order of newly written documents changes, and nothing
    compares the text.
    
    Automated:
    
    ```
    ./mvnw clean install -Pdev
    ./mvnw dependency:tree -Dincludes='com.googlecode.json-simple:*'   # expect no matches
    ```
    
    Manually, against a running instance, the paths that changed shape:
    
    * create and update a series through the External API, and check that
    `subjects` come back as a list
    of plain strings and that empty fields are still present as `""` / `[]`
    / `{}`
    * the admin UI event and series detail views, in particular
    `access.json` for both, which splices ACL
      documents built with jettison into a Gson response
    * a bulk update of several events
    * the statistics endpoints, admin and External API
    * the workflow and scheduler REST endpoints
    
    **Signed URL playback deserves a specific check.** With URL signing
    configured, confirm that a URL
    signed by Opencast plays, that a tampered signature is rejected and that
    an expired policy is
    rejected. If you have a third-party signer, confirm it still produces an
    accepted signature: the
    policy for a resource valid until `1893456000000` must render as
    
    ```json
    {"Statement":{"Condition":{"DateLessThan":1893456000000},"Resource":"http:\/\/localhost:8080\/files\/mediapackage\/abc\/def\/test.mp4"}}
    ```
    
    with the forward slashes escaped, base64url encoded into `policy` and
    HMAC-SHA256 hex encoded into
    `signature`.
    
    ### AI Usage
    
    Claude was used extensively for this pull request, driven interactively
    rather than autonomously.
    
    It carried out the file-by-file migration, wrote the commit messages,
    and did the investigation behind
    the findings listed above — including bisecting the
    `AccessControlParser` regression to a typo in a
    test fixture, identifying the `ClassCastException` in the remote series
    service, and tracing the
    transitive json-simple and leaked JUnit dependencies exposed by the
    removal.
    
    Behavioural claims about the two libraries were checked by running them
    side by side rather than
    asserted from memory, and every commit was verified with a build and the
    relevant module's tests. The
    whole branch was verified with a full build and test run, and against a
    running Opencast, including
    the signed URL checks above.
    
